# Line endings — enforce LF everywhere.
#
# Every tracked file in this repo is LF today (226/226 at the time of writing) and the CI
# checks compare generated output byte-for-byte, so a stray CRLF shows up as a whole-file
# diff or a failed sync check rather than as anything obviously line-ending-related.
#
# Without this file the outcome depends on each contributor's `core.autocrlf`, which is a
# per-machine setting nobody sets deliberately: a tool that rewrites a file on Windows can
# hand back CRLF and the next commit silently renormalises. Declaring it here makes the
# convention a property of the repo instead of a property of whoever cloned it.
#
# `text=auto` still auto-detects binary content and leaves it untouched, so this stays safe
# if images or archives are added later.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Scripts where CRLF is not a cosmetic problem.
# A shell script with CRLF fails on a Linux runner with the notoriously unhelpful
# "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M" — the .sh files here run in GitHub Actions.
*.sh  text eol=lf

# Kept explicit because these are executed rather than merely read, and both generators
# must agree byte-for-byte between the .ps1 and .sh implementations.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.py  text eol=lf

# Generated — regenerate with scripts/generate-agents-md.ps1 (or .sh), never hand-edit.
# Marking it collapses the diff by default on GitHub and keeps review attention on the
# agent/skill frontmatter that actually drives it.
AGENTS.md linguist-generated=true
